>
  <p>A good study group is one of the most efficient ways to learn. A bad one is a two-hour conversation about everything except the exam, with a textbook open as an alibi. The difference is entirely in how you run it.</p>

  <h2 id="why">When groups actually help</h2>
  <p>The research on cooperative learning, reviewed by Johnson and Johnson and by Springer and colleagues, consistently favours well-structured group work over solo study for many outcomes. The active ingredient is explaining: Fiorella and Mayer show that teaching material, and even just expecting to teach it, deepens your own understanding. A group gives you people to teach.</p>

  <h2 id="setup">Set the group up right</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Three to five committed people</strong> at a similar level and pace.</li>
    <li><strong>A clear goal per session:</strong> "work through chapter 6 problems", not "revise maths".</li>
    <li><strong>A fixed length</strong> with a start and stop time.</li>
    <li><strong>Prep required.</strong> Everyone arrives having done the reading, so the time is spent on the hard parts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="run">Run a useful session</h2>
  <p>Rotate who teaches each topic, so everyone has to explain something from memory. Quiz each other with questions prepared in advance. Work problems on a shared board where mistakes are visible and can be discussed. Keep it moving with a <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">timer</a>, and agree the agenda beforehand using the <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> so no one spends the first twenty minutes deciding what to do.</p>

  <h2 id="avoid">Avoid the traps</h2>
  <p>Watch for social drift, one person doing all the work while others coast, and groups that meet to feel productive rather than to produce. A clear agenda, prep, and a hard finish time fix most of it. For shared assignments, split the project into dated, owned steps with the <a href="/tools/assignment-planner/">assignment planner</a>. And remember to do your own solo testing too; see <a href="/guides/evidence-based-study-techniques/">active recall and spaced repetition</a>.</p>
</Guide>
